                        "severity": "Critical",
                        "reasoning": "The class implements Serializable and defines a private readObject(ObjectInputStream) method. In Java, this method is invoked automatically during deserialization. The code calls stream.defaultReadObject(), which populates taskAction from attacker-controlled serialized data. It then uses taskAction to decide whether to execute a system command and calls Runtime.getRuntime().exec(taskAction). Because taskAction is not validated beyond a prefix check (\"sleep\"/\"ping\") and a length limit, an attacker can craft a serialized object that passes these checks and triggers command execution during deserialization, resulting in remote code execution or arbitrary command execution depending on the platform and command parsing.",
                        "mitigation": "Avoid executing commands during deserialization. Remove/disable custom readObject logic and do not implement Serializable for objects that may be deserialized from untrusted sources. If deserialization is required, use a safe serialization mechanism (e.g., JSON with strict schema validation) or enforce an ObjectInputFilter to whitelist allowed classes and block others. Additionally, never pass attacker-controlled strings to Runtime.exec; if command execution is unavoidable, use a fixed allowlist of commands and build ProcessBuilder with separate arguments (no shell interpretation) and strict parameter validation.",
