                        "reasoning": "The class stores the Spring ApplicationContext in a static field (context) and exposes it via a public static method (getEnv()). Any code running in the same JVM can call EnvironmentExposure.getEnv() and obtain the Spring Environment. This can unintentionally broaden access to configuration/secret material (e.g., environment variables, property sources) beyond what the application intends to expose. Because the field is static and globally accessible, it also increases the risk of misuse in tests, other components, or any code that should not have access to environment configuration. While this file does not show direct secret logging or injection, the security-relevant concern is that it creates a global access point to potentially sensitive configuration data.",
