                        "cwe": "CWE-287",
                        "severity": "Medium",
                        "reasoning": "The endpoint /crypto/encoding/basic returns a Basic-Auth header value derived from a password chosen server-side and stored in the HTTP session under the key \"basicAuth\". The completion endpoint /crypto/encoding/basic-auth then treats the presence of that session attribute as the basis for authentication/verification: it compares the client-supplied answer_user/answer_pwd (request parameters) by recomputing Basic auth and checking equality with the session-stored value.\n\nThis is security-relevant because the server is not validating credentials against a trusted identity store; instead it relies on a session attribute that can be influenced if an attacker can obtain or fixate a victim's session (session fixation) or otherwise cause the victim to use an attacker-controlled session. In such cases, the attacker could potentially set up a session where \"basicAuth\" corresponds to attacker-chosen credentials, and then pass matching answer_user/answer_pwd to satisfy the equality check.\n\nAdditionally, the code uses request.getSession() (not shown whether it creates a new session) and stores the computed secret in session, which increases the impact of any session compromise.",
                        "mitigation": "Avoid using session-stored Basic-Auth material as the sole basis for credential verification. Instead, validate answer_user/answer_pwd against a server-side secret or user store directly in the POST handler, without relying on a session attribute that can be targeted via session fixation/compromise. If session state is required for the lesson, ensure session fixation protections are enabled (e.g., session ID rotation on authentication), consider using a one-time nonce/token bound to the user and request, and do not store reusable credential material in session. Also consider using constant-time comparison for secrets (e.g., MessageDigest.isEqual) to reduce timing leakage.",
