                    "properties": {
                        "cwe": "CWE-285",
                        "severity": "Critical",
                        "reasoning": "The controller exposes three state-changing and data-access endpoints: GET /mail, POST /mail, and DELETE /mail. There are no method/class-level security annotations (e.g., @PreAuthorize) and no explicit authorization logic in the methods. \n\nFor POST /mail, the code persists the client-supplied Email object directly via mailboxRepository.save(email) without verifying that the caller is allowed to send to the specified recipient or that the recipient matches the authenticated user. This enables a caller to create emails for arbitrary recipients (trusting client-controlled fields).\n\nFor DELETE /mail, the code deletes all mailbox records via mailboxRepository.deleteAll() without checking the caller\u2019s identity/permissions. Any caller that can invoke this endpoint can wipe the entire mailbox dataset.\n\nFor GET /mail, the code uses authentication.getName() when authentication is non-null, otherwise it uses the literal \"anonymous\". There is no enforcement that the caller is authenticated, so an unauthenticated caller can query the mailbox for recipient \"anonymous\" (and potentially other users if the repository method is not strictly scoped, though the username is derived from authentication). The primary authorization gap is the absence of any access control on the endpoints themselves.",
                        "mitigation": "Add authorization controls to all endpoints. For example:\n- Require authentication for GET/POST/DELETE (e.g., configure Spring Security to require authenticated users for /mail, or add @PreAuthorize(\"isAuthenticated()\") / @PreAuthorize checks).\n- For POST /mail, ignore/override client-controlled recipient fields and set them from the authenticated principal (e.g., email.setRecipient(authentication.getName())) or validate that the authenticated user is allowed to use the provided recipient.\n- For DELETE /mail, restrict deletion to the authenticated user\u2019s own mailbox (e.g., mailboxRepository.deleteByRecipient(authentication.getName())) and/or require an admin role.\n- Consider using object-level authorization (e.g., @PreAuthorize with recipient checks) rather than repository-wide operations.",
