                    "properties": {
                        "cwe": "CWE-285",
                        "severity": "High",
                        "reasoning": "The method allowedTrace(HttpExchange t, String username) decides whether to show stored HTTP request traces to the current user using only String.contains() checks on the traced request URI path and query. This is fragile: an attacker could craft or influence traced URIs such that the username appears as a substring in the path/query (or the reversed username appears in the query) without actually belonging to that user, causing allowed to remain true and exposing other users' traces. Additionally, using contains() on the raw query string (not parsed parameters) makes it easy for unrelated parameters or values to satisfy the checks. The code also defaults allowed=true and only blocks in these two specific patterns, so any other trace types are always shown to the user.",
                        "mitigation": "Implement robust, parameterized authorization checks based on explicit ownership/tenant identifiers stored alongside the trace (e.g., trace.getOwnerUsername() or trace.getUserId()) rather than inferring from URI substrings. If URI-based filtering is unavoidable, parse the URI path and query into structured components and compare exact values (e.g., equals on specific query parameter values) with proper normalization/encoding handling. Also default to deny unless ownership can be verified.",
