                    "properties": {
                        "cwe": "CWE-95",
                        "severity": "High",
                        "reasoning": "The file defines `req.exec` as `return eval(text)`. If an attacker can influence the `text` argument passed to `req.exec`, this becomes arbitrary JavaScript execution in the browser context (or Node context if used there). RequireJS typically uses this for executing module factory code, but the presence of a raw `eval` means any upstream injection into the evaluated string would be exploitable.",
                        "mitigation": "Avoid using `eval` by using RequireJS build/optimizer outputs that do not require runtime string evaluation, or configure the loader to load precompiled modules. If you must keep RequireJS, ensure that module code and any `text` passed to `req.exec` cannot be influenced by untrusted input (e.g., strict CSP, integrity checks, and do not accept module definitions from user-controlled sources).",
                        "confidence": 0.86,
